! Half-wave rectifier driven by the FIRST-CLASS diode circuit element,
! "Component Type = String Diode", in a transient circuit-coupled
! magnetodynamics run.
!
! WHY THIS TEST EXISTS
! --------------------
! circuits2D_transient_diode_rectifier models the very same diode as a
! "Component Type = Resistor" whose Resistance is a MATC expression of the
! EXPORTED circuit variable "crt v 2". That test validates the generic Picard
! mechanism. THIS test validates the built-in Diode component, which
!   * reads its own branch voltage internally (from the relaxed latest coupled
!     iterate held by CircuitsAndDynamics), so it needs NO
!     "Export Circuit Variables" and no MATC string -- note the absence of that
!     keyword from Solver 1 below, which is part of what is being tested;
!   * carries the sign convention itself (forward bias is V > Vf);
!   * hard-fails in the harmonic solver instead of silently producing an empty
!     voltage row.
! Physics, mesh, circuit topology, drive and solver settings are IDENTICAL to
! circuits2D_transient_diode_rectifier, and the diode parameters below are the
! same numbers that sif's MATC expression used (with Vf = 0), so the SAME
! independently computed reference value must come out. The two tests together
! therefore also assert that the component reproduces the keyword-driven model
! exactly.
!
! CIRCUIT
!   ideal sinusoidal source -- stranded FEM coil (component 1) -- diode (2)
! all in series. The coil is given an explicit "Resistance = Real 1.0", which
! makes Add_stranded skip the sigma-based FEM resistance, so the coil
! contributes exactly 1 ohm plus its (linear, constant) inductance.
!
! DIODE MODEL (built in, smoothed, VOLTAGE controlled)
!   R(V) = Ron + 0.5*(Roff - Ron)*(1 - TANH((V - Vf)/Vs))
! with V the component's OWN branch voltage. Forward bias is V > Vf. tanh is
! used rather than the algebraic sigmoid V/sqrt(V^2+Vs^2): the algebraic form
! has a 1/V^2 tail, so R - Ron = Roff*Vs^2/(4V^2) would still be ~390 ohm at the
! ~0.7 V forward drop reached here. The tanh tail decays as Roff*exp(-2V/Vs) and
! gives R = 0.178 ohm at the peak.
! Ron = 0.1, Roff = 1e5 (kept <= ~1e6 to protect the conditioning of the linear
! system and of the Picard map; the resulting leakage V/(Roff+Rcoil) ~ 1e-4 A is
! asserted by the sibling circuits2D_transient_diode_component_blocking),
! Vs = 0.1 V = 1% of V0, Vf = 0 (an ideal-threshold diode, matching the MATC
! model of the gate test so that the reference value carries over).
!
! PICARD TUNING. The component is stamped as R(V_relaxed)*I - V = 0 with R
! frozen at the latest relaxed iterate -- Picard, no Newton term. The relaxation
! factor below is the one measured on the gate test (sweep documented in
! ../circuits2D_transient_diode_rectifier/sif/diode_rectifier.sif): undamped
! Picard diverges at every timestep, w = 0.15 is the stability limit, w = 0.10
! gives 0 non-converged timesteps at median 7 / max 30 iterations per step.
!
! LINEAR SYSTEM: a DIRECT solve is used deliberately -- with an iterative solver
! the blocking-phase field norm sits at the level of the iterative solve's own
! noise and the coupled loop never converges there. STEADY STATE CONVERGENCE IS
! ABSOLUTE, because the A-field norm passes through ~0 at t = 5.000 s where a
! relative criterion is 0/0. Both are explained in full in the gate test sif.
!
! REFERENCE VALUE
! Unchanged from circuits2D_transient_diode_rectifier: an independent lumped
! BDF1 solver (../circuits2D_transient_diode_rectifier/diode_reference.py, no
! code in common with Elmer) that solves the implicit relation for V_D by
! bisection at every step. Forward peak of the SECOND period, step 142,
! t = 7.10 s:
!   I_diode = 3.9463754144 A,  V_diode = +0.703095 V,  R_diode = 0.178162 ohm
!
! The asserted norm is "res: i_component(2)", the SOLVED circuit dof published
! by CircuitsOutput. It is deliberately not "crt i 2": under relaxation the
! exported mesh copy of a circuit variable that controls no keyword is a
! first-order filter of the true iterate (pole 1-w) and still lags by O(0.1%)
! when the loop exits. Here nothing is exported at all, which is the point.
!
! Based on circuits2D_transient_variable_resistor by Eelis Takala, Trafotek Oy.
Check Keywords "Warn"
INCLUDE sif/diode_component_rectifier_circuit.definitions
INCLUDE 1381/mesh.names
Header 1
   Mesh DB "." "1381"
End

$ V0 = 10        ! source amplitude [V]
$ f  = 0.2       ! source frequency [Hz], chosen so that wL = 2.74*(Rcoil+Ron)
$ T  = 1/f
$ nper = 100     ! timesteps per period
$ dt = T/nper
$ nsteps = 142   ! last step lands exactly on the forward peak of period 2
$ wrel = 0.10    ! Circuit Variable Relaxation Factor, see PICARD TUNING above
$ sstol = 1.0e-10

Simulation 1
   Max Output Level = 5
   Coordinate System = Axi symmetric
   Simulation Type = Transient
   Timestepping Method = BDF
   BDF Order = 1
   Timestep Sizes(1) = $ dt
   Timestep Intervals(1) = $ nsteps
!  > 1 enables the within-timestep Picard loop over the nonlinear resistance.
   Steady State Max Iterations = 100
!  Guards against a premature exit while the field is nearly zero (blocking
!  phase and the two zero crossings), where a single coupled iteration can look
!  converged before the nonlinearity has been iterated at all.
   Steady State Min Iterations = 3
End
Solver 1
   Exec Solver = Always
   Equation = Circuits
   Procedure = "CircuitsAndDynamics" "CircuitsAndDynamics"
!  NOTE: "Export Circuit Variables" is deliberately NOT set. The Diode component
!  reads its own branch voltage from the solver's internal relaxed iterate, so
!  the circuit variables never have to be published as mesh variables.
   Circuit Variable Relaxation Factor = Real $ wrel
End
Solver 2
   Equation = "Mag"
   Variable = A
   Procedure = "MagnetoDynamics2D" "MagnetoDynamics2D"
   NonLinear System Relaxation Factor=1
   Export Lagrange Multiplier = Logical True
   Linear System Solver = Direct
   Linear System Direct Method = UMFPACK
   Steady State Convergence Tolerance = $ sstol
   Steady State Convergence Absolute = Logical True
End
Solver 3 ! Result output
   Exec Solver = Never
   Equation = "ResultOutput"
   Procedure = "ResultOutputSolve" "ResultOutputSolver"
   Output File Name = 1381-results
   Vtu format = Logical True
   Save Geometry Ids = Logical True
End
Solver 4
   Exec Solver = Always
   Equation = Circuits Output
   Procedure = "CircuitsAndDynamics" "CircuitsOutput"
End
Solver 5
   Exec Solver = After Timestep
   Equation = "sv"
   Procedure = "SaveData" "SaveScalars"
   Filename = 1381/dat/diode_component_rectifier.dat
!  No "Variable N" entries here: with no circuit export there are no "crt i" /
!  "crt v" mesh variables to save. The asserted quantity is the solved circuit
!  dof published by CircuitsOutput.
   Show Norm Name = String "res: i_component(2)"
End
Equation 1
   Active Solvers(2) = 2 3
End
Equation 2
   Active Solvers(3) = 1 2 3
End
Material 1
   Name = iron
   Electric Conductivity = 0
   Relative Permeability = 2000
End
Material 2
   Name = air
   Electric Conductivity = 0
   Relative Permeability = 1
End
Material 3
   Name = aluminium
   Relative Permeability = 1
   Electric Conductivity = 1e7
End
Body 1
   Name = Core
   Target Bodies(6) = 1 2 3 4 5 6
   Equation = 1
   Material = 1
End
Body 2
   Name = air
   Target Bodies(2) = 9 7
   Equation = 1
   Material = 2
End
Body 3
   Name = test 1
   Target Bodies = 8
   Equation = 2
   Material = 3
End
Component 1
   Name = String test inductor
   Master Bodies = Integer 3
   Coil Type = String stranded
   Number of Turns = Real 1000
!  Explicit coil resistance: suppresses the FEM sigma-based resistance so that
!  the loop resistance is exactly 1 ohm + R_diode.
   Resistance = Real 1.0
End
Component 2
   Name = String diode
!  The first-class diode. No Master Bodies (it is a pure lumped element), no
!  MATC expression and no reference to any exported variable.
   Component Type = String Diode
   Diode Forward Voltage = Real 0.0
   Diode On Resistance = Real 0.1
   Diode Off Resistance = Real 1.0e5
   Diode Smoothing Voltage = Real 0.1
End
Body Force 1
   Name = "Circuit"
   testsource = Variable time
     Real MATC "V0*sin(2*pi*f*tx)"
End
Boundary Condition 1
   Target Boundaries(4) = $ center0 center1 center2 center3
   A = Real 0
End
Boundary Condition 2
   Target Boundaries(2) = 4 6
   Name = "Air Gap"
   Air Gap = logical True
   Air Gap Length = Real 0.0001
   Air Gap Relative Permeability = Real 1
End
Boundary Condition 3
   Target Boundaries(7) = 10 13 14 17 18 19 27
   Infinity BC = Logical True
End

Solver 2 :: Reference Norm = 9.86655070E-03
! Independent lumped BDF1 reference at t = 7.10 s, see REFERENCE VALUE above --
! the same number the MATC-based gate test asserts.
! 2e-2 leaves room for round-off differences in the tail of tanh across
! platforms.
Solver 5 :: Reference Norm = 3.9463754144
Solver 5 :: Reference Norm Tolerance = Real 2E-02
